WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.890
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:04.530
In this lesson, we're gonna take a look

3
00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:08.250
at the World Partition tool that we have for Unreal.

4
00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.349
Now, again, this is gonna be a bit of an overview

5
00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:12.189
and not a full deep dive

6
00:00:12.189 --> 00:00:15.570
as we do have classes covering this in more detail.

7
00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.929
So let's get started.

8
00:00:18.929 --> 00:00:21.129
So how it works.

9
00:00:21.129 --> 00:00:22.609
So we're gonna be opening up

10
00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:26.010
the World Partition EXE project.

11
00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.429
I'm giving you two projects

12
00:00:27.429 --> 00:00:29.289
in this particular group of classes.

13
00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:31.449
One of them was in the world that we were building

14
00:00:31.469 --> 00:00:34.670
and then this one takes a bit of our mini dark ruins

15
00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:38.429
and puts it into a world with a World Partition set up.

16
00:00:38.429 --> 00:00:43.429
So the World Partition divides your world to a grid

17
00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.810
and you can adjust the settings to that grid

18
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.729
to when and how they load.

19
00:00:47.729 --> 00:00:49.770
And you can think of it as virtual texturing

20
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.770
but this is virtual level building

21
00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:55.490
so that the most important parts of your world

22
00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.009
are loading where your player is at

23
00:00:58.009 --> 00:01:00.049
and that's kind of what you can do

24
00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.109
and you can manage that controlling your cell side

25
00:01:03.109 --> 00:01:04.909
to load the level in smaller sections

26
00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:07.230
for interactive flow and customization.

27
00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:08.629
So it's really great.

28
00:01:08.629 --> 00:01:11.549
It's easier to make larger worlds with it

29
00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:12.670
as it handles the data,

30
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.750
keeping things compartmentalized and loaded

31
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.870
as needed with the grid system.

32
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.390
So this grid system is very beneficial

33
00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:23.469
and makes it so you can control via sections

34
00:01:23.469 --> 00:01:25.430
and how things are to be loaded.

35
00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.459
It's pretty awesome.

36
00:01:27.459 --> 00:01:32.879
So how it works also is the open world default map type

37
00:01:32.879 --> 00:01:34.780
is designed to be a starting point

38
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.780
for creating large open world maps

39
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.159
and comes with the following features enabled by default.

40
00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:45.019
You have World Partition,

41
00:01:45.019 --> 00:01:46.980
you have the one file per actor

42
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.459
to streamline saving assets

43
00:01:49.459 --> 00:01:54.120
so there's no waiting for checking with version control

44
00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.640
and you also have data layers

45
00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.359
and you can dynamically load and unload layers

46
00:01:58.359 --> 00:02:01.219
in the editor to help manage complex worlds.

47
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.640
So not only are you working with World Partition,

48
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.000
you can have data layers

49
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.680
as well as hierarchical levels of detail.

50
00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:10.960
Kind of what we've talked about HLODs before

51
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.419
but these are HLODs specifically catered

52
00:02:13.419 --> 00:02:17.909
to the world building World Partition tool.

53
00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:20.669
It's used to control the calculation of assets

54
00:02:20.669 --> 00:02:23.509
so you can bundle assets for quicker calculations.

55
00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:26.349
All of these are set up to help streamlining production

56
00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:27.830
for larger worlds.

57
00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:29.870
So let's go and see how that works

58
00:02:29.889 --> 00:02:32.629
and we're going to punch in a couple of CVars

59
00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:37.520
to see that in real time.

60
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.599
So you can see right now we're in the world

61
00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:40.599
and we're looking around

62
00:02:40.599 --> 00:02:42.580
in the environment that I've given you.

63
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.919
You want to make sure when you do start out

64
00:02:44.919 --> 00:02:46.879
or grab any kind of level,

65
00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:49.439
double check to make sure you have enabled streaming.

66
00:02:49.439 --> 00:02:50.599
This is really important

67
00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:53.759
if you're grabbing a set of levels from the marketplace

68
00:02:53.759 --> 00:02:55.860
and you want to use World Partition,

69
00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.319
enable streaming is going to be very important.

70
00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:00.520
You'll notice there's a runtime hash class

71
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.639
that we can choose.

72
00:03:01.659 --> 00:03:03.900
The default that I have on this particular map

73
00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.259
that I've given you has the hash set

74
00:03:06.259 --> 00:03:10.379
but the newer one or the newer set of data calculation

75
00:03:10.379 --> 00:03:13.659
that we have is a World Partition runtime spatial hash.

76
00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:16.099
So I do recommend using that one when possible.

77
00:03:16.099 --> 00:03:19.500
For purposes of just us working in class

78
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.180
I'm going to leave it as hash set

79
00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.819
but again this one is a little bit more streamlined

80
00:03:23.819 --> 00:03:27.159
and a little bit more up to date and is a better value

81
00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:29.740
because it is a latest and greatest

82
00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.060
but it's really up to you

83
00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:32.560
and both do work great

84
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.629
but this one is the newest kid on the block.

85
00:03:36.629 --> 00:03:40.030
So another thing we can do to see how this looks like

